\chapter{The shape of a working week, the \texttt{cli.utils.workweek}
module}%
\label{cli.utils.workweek}

Three commands need to know when the user is \emph{supposed} to be
working.  [[nytid todo]] slots pending tasks into work hours,
[[nytid schedule show]] computes the free slots of each work day, and
[[nytid track stats]] compares the time actually tracked with the hours
a work week is expected to contain.  All three must agree on what a
work day is: a user who removes Friday from the mask should see that
Friday disappear from the schedule \emph{and} stop being counted as a
day on which eight hours were due.

The settings originally lived in the [[todo]] module, and [[schedule]]
imported them from there.  [[track]] cannot do the same, because
[[todo]] imports from [[track]] at module level (it derives times for
started todos from the tracking history), so an import in the other
direction is a cycle.  A neutral home under [[cli.utils]] resolves
this: the settings depend on nothing but the [[default]] helper
(\cref{cli.utils.defaults}), and every command imports them from here.

The configuration keys keep their historical [[todo.]] prefix.  They
are settings users have already written into their configuration
files, and renaming a key would silently reset every configured work
week to the built-in default.  The prefix therefore records where the
settings came from, not which command owns them.

\section{Module structure}

<<[[workweek.py]]>>=
"""The user's work week, shared by the todo, schedule and track commands.

Work hours and work days are read from the personal config through
`default`; the keys keep their historical `todo.` prefix. See the
`cli.utils.workweek` chapter in the manual for the design rationale.
"""
import datetime

from nytid.cli.utils.defaults import default

<<configuration keys and defaults>>
<<work-window accessors>>
<<work-day predicates>>
@

\section{The work window}

A work day is described by three values: when it starts, when it ends,
and which days of the week count.  Start and end are kept as
[[HH:MM]] strings rather than parsed here, because the two consumers
parse them differently---[[schedule]] combines them with a date into
[[datetime]] objects, while [[todo]] only ever displays them---and a
string round-trips through the configuration file unchanged.

<<configuration keys and defaults>>=
WORK_START_CONFIG = "todo.work_start"
WORK_END_CONFIG = "todo.work_end"
WORK_DAYS_CONFIG = "todo.work_days"

DEFAULT_WORK_START = "08:00"
DEFAULT_WORK_END = "17:00"
DEFAULT_WORK_DAYS = "mon,tue,wed,thu,fri"
@

The accessors are functions rather than module-level constants so that
the configuration is consulted when a command runs, not when the module
is imported.  Tests rely on this: they can patch [[default]] and observe
the effect without reloading the module.

<<work-window accessors>>=
def get_work_start() -> str:
    """Get configured work day start time as `HH:MM`."""
    return default(WORK_START_CONFIG, DEFAULT_WORK_START, str)


def get_work_end() -> str:
    """Get configured work day end time as `HH:MM`."""
    return default(WORK_END_CONFIG, DEFAULT_WORK_END, str)
@

The day mask accepts two spellings.  Written by hand, the natural form
is the comma-separated string above; written by a tool, or by a user
who prefers structure, it is a JSON list of names.  Normalising both to
one lower-case list means that no consumer needs to know which form the
file used, and that [[Mon]] and [[mon]] name the same day.

<<work-window accessors>>=
def get_work_days() -> list[str]:
    """Get configured work days as a list of lower-case day names.

    Handles both string (`"mon,tue,wed"`) and list
    (`["mon", "tue", "wed"]`) config formats.
    """
    days_val = default(WORK_DAYS_CONFIG, DEFAULT_WORK_DAYS)
    if isinstance(days_val, list):
        days_val = ",".join(str(d) for d in days_val)
    return [d.strip().lower() for d in days_val.split(",")]
@

Let's verify that both spellings normalise to the same list.  We patch
[[default]] rather than writing a configuration file, since the only
thing under test is the normalisation.

<<test functions>>=
def test_get_work_days_accepts_string_and_list(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    "nytid.cli.utils.workweek.default",
    lambda key, fallback, cast=None: "Mon, tue,WED",
  )
  assert get_work_days() == ["mon", "tue", "wed"]

  monkeypatch.setattr(
    "nytid.cli.utils.workweek.default",
    lambda key, fallback, cast=None: ["mon", "Tue"],
  )
  assert get_work_days() == ["mon", "tue"]
@

\section{Naming the days}

The mask names days the way people do, [[mon]] through [[sun]], while
Python numbers them the way [[date.weekday()]] does, Monday as zero.
One list, indexed by that number, is the whole translation.

<<configuration keys and defaults>>=
DAY_NAMES = ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri", "sat", "sun"]
@

The predicate takes the mask as an optional argument.  This is not a
convenience: [[schedule]] reads the work window once per invocation
and its tests control the mask by patching that single read, so a
predicate that consulted the configuration on its own would silently
escape the tests' control.  A caller with no mask of its own, such as
[[track]], omits the argument and gets the configured one.

<<work-day predicates>>=
def is_work_day(date, work_days=None) -> bool:
    """Return whether `date` falls on a configured work day.

    `work_days` is a list of lower-case day names as returned by
    `get_work_days()`; it is read from the configuration when omitted.
    """
    if work_days is None:
        work_days = get_work_days()
    return DAY_NAMES[date.weekday()] in work_days
@

Counting the work days in a date range is what turns a mask into an
expectation: so many work days times so many hours per day.  Both ends
are inclusive, because the ranges this is used for are calendar
periods---a week from Monday to Sunday, a month from the first to the
last---and those are naturally named by their end points.  An inverted
range counts nothing rather than raising, so that a caller clipping a
period to \enquote{today} need not special-case a period that has not
started.

<<work-day predicates>>=
def count_work_days(start_date, end_date, work_days=None) -> int:
    """Count the work days from `start_date` to `end_date`, inclusive.

    Returns 0 when `end_date` precedes `start_date`.
    """
    if work_days is None:
        work_days = get_work_days()
    count = 0
    date = start_date
    while date <= end_date:
        if is_work_day(date, work_days):
            count += 1
        date += datetime.timedelta(days=1)
    return count
@

Let's verify the predicates against a known week: 2026-09-07 is a
Monday.  The explicit mask must win over the configuration, and the
inclusive count over a full week must be exactly the five weekdays.

<<test functions>>=
def test_is_work_day_uses_the_explicit_mask():
  monday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 7)
  saturday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 12)
  assert is_work_day(monday, ["mon"])
  assert not is_work_day(saturday, ["mon"])
  assert is_work_day(saturday, ["sat"])


def test_is_work_day_falls_back_to_the_configuration(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    "nytid.cli.utils.workweek.get_work_days",
    lambda: ["sat"],
  )
  assert is_work_day(datetime.date(2026, 9, 12))
  assert not is_work_day(datetime.date(2026, 9, 7))


def test_count_work_days_is_inclusive():
  monday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 7)
  sunday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 13)
  weekdays = ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri"]
  assert count_work_days(monday, sunday, weekdays) == 5
  assert count_work_days(monday, monday, weekdays) == 1
  saturday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 12)
  assert count_work_days(saturday, saturday, weekdays) == 0


def test_count_work_days_of_inverted_range_is_zero():
  later = datetime.date(2026, 9, 13)
  earlier = datetime.date(2026, 9, 7)
  assert count_work_days(later, earlier, ["mon"]) == 0
@

